config BR2_PACKAGE_REGLINUX_BLUETOOTH
	bool "reglinux-bluetooth"

	select BR2_PACKAGE_BLUEZ5_UTILS
	# btmon: HCI tracing, the only tool that shows why a pairing failed. It
	# sits below both regmsgd and bluetoothd, so it is also the independent
	# second opinion that bluetoothctl used to provide. No dependencies.
	select BR2_PACKAGE_BLUEZ5_UTILS_MONITOR
	# hciattach and hcitool, needed by S30bluetooth and wiimote3rdPartyConnect.
	# DEPRECATED depends on CLIENT || TOOLS; CLIENT is gone (see below), so
	# TOOLS is what keeps hciattach in the image -- dropping both silently
	# removes the per-board firmware attach on all 8 board families.
	select BR2_PACKAGE_BLUEZ5_UTILS_TOOLS
	select BR2_PACKAGE_BLUEZ5_UTILS_DEPRECATED
	select BR2_PACKAGE_BLUEZ5_UTILS_PLUGINS_SIXAXIS # sixaxis for PS3 controllers
	select BR2_PACKAGE_UTIL_LINUX_RFKILL
	help
	  Bluetooth hardware bring-up: the per-board hciattach recipe in
	  S30bluetooth, the org.bluez D-Bus policy, and the BlueZ packages.

	  Everything above the controller -- bluetoothd's lifecycle, the pairing
	  agent, discovery and the device operations -- lives in regmsgd.

	  Dropped when that moved in, all unreferenced afterwards:
	    BLUEZ_TOOLS              bt-agent and friends, 1.0 MB; bt-agent is
	                             exactly what regmsgd's Agent1 replaced
	    BLUEZ5_UTILS_TEST        34 Python scripts, 183 KB; keeping Python in
	                             this path is what the migration removed

	  CLIENT (bluetoothctl, 479 KB) is dropped for a second reason. It is the
	  only thing that makes bluez probe for readline: configure.ac enables the
	  READLINE conditional just for the client, and Makefile.am compiles
	  src/shared/shell.c -- the sole caller of rl_message() -- only under it.
	  readline.h declares that as `extern int rl_message ();`, and C23 reads
	  empty parens as "no parameters", so GCC 16 rejects the call. Without
	  CLIENT, shell.c never compiles, and that is what had Bluetooth and
	  xpadneo switched off on K3 and TH1520 in reglinux-system/Config.in --
	  both are selected there again now.

	  Its three real uses are now regmsg commands: `bluetooth pairable`
	  (the OrangePi step in S30bluetooth), `bluetooth info` and
	  `bluetooth devices`.
